  server, no build tooling, no tests.

  That matters a lot, because a notification system is mostly [3mplumbing between things that already exist[0m: it needs a
  task entity, a notion of who a user is, a source of change events, a subscription concept ("tasks they care about"),
  and a delivery channel. None of those are here. So before I ask about notification design, I need to know what I'm
  actually building against.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether "this app" means this repo, or a real app that lives somewhere else and this directory is a stub.
  The answer changes everything downstream — it decides whether the spec covers tasks + users + events + delivery (four
  subsystems, which I'd want to decompose and sequence) or just the notification layer against interfaces that already
  exist.

  - [1mReal app is elsewhere[0m — cheapest and most focused. I design only notifications, against your existing task/user
    model and event sources. I'd need to see that code or its API. Rules out nothing.
  - [1mBuild everything here[0m — the honest read of the repo as-is. This is genuinely 3-4 subsystems; I'd want to decompose
    and spec the first slice rather than write one giant spec. Slowest path, but nothing is assumed.
  - [1mGreenfield, notifications-first[0m — treat tasks/users as thin stubs I define minimally, just enough to hang
    notifications on. Fast, but the stubs become de-facto architecture and are expensive to rework later.

  My recommendation is the first if it's true — tell me where the real app is. If this repo really is the app, I'd take
  the second over the third: a notification system built on placeholder entities tends to bake in the wrong event model,
  and that's the expensive part to redo.
